  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe next few days will be active for thunderstorms, especially \nSouthwest of a line from Syracuse down to the Catskills. That is\nwhere many of the models have the warm front stalling so along \nand north of the front, more stable air will lower the severe \nrisk but increase the heavy rain threat. Early this morning, \nwater vapor imagery is showing an initial shortwave passing \nthrough leading to some rain showers. This has been well \ndepicted in the models and helps lead so some uncertainty with \nstorm chances this afternoon around peak heating when the severe\ningredients are in place. Many of the models do not have a \nshortwave passage this afternoon and water vapor imagery does \nnot show anything of note upstream for a ways. Even though we \nmay see between 2000 and 3000 J/kg of SBCAPE and 30 to 40 knots \nof bulk shear this afternoon, we may not have a trigger to \ndevelop the deep convection. Still plenty of CAMs have some \nprecipitation developing with a speckling of 40 dbz cores in the\nHREF. The front that is near by may be enough to get some \ninitial convection near it and outflow boundaries are able to be\nthe needed trigger for storms. \n\nIf storms do develop today, the large cape and dry air aloft \nwill predominantly have a hail and wet microburst threat. There \nis a little bit of curvature in the forecast hodographs for this\nafternoon as well so an isolated tornado cannot be ruled out, \nespecially closer to where the warm front is stalled. \n\nTomorrow is another difficult day to pin down as a marine layer\nis trying to push in from the west that could help at least \nstabilize the boundary layer. The more stable air is likely east\nof I-81 but farther west, it does look like there will be \nenough sun to become unstable. Along the edge of this marine \nlayer will need to be watched closely as there will be enhanced \nenvironmental helicity. Any storms with supercell \ncharacteristics that form in the afternoon and cross from the \nunstable air into the marine layer may briefly pose a tornado \nrisk. Shear tomorrow is also looking better today with most \nmodels showing 50 to 55 knots of Bulk Shear. More clouds may \nlimit the overall CAPE to around 1000 to 1500 west of I-81 but \nthat is still plenty to see organized convection. The timing of \na subtle shortwave also looks better for storms to fire in the \nafternoon around or just after peak heating so if the stable \nmarine layer does not push west through the entire region in the\nmorning, there is higher confidence in more widespread severe \nWednesday.\n\nThursday is not quite out of the woods yet for severe \nthunderstorms as low level moisture remains in place for part of\nthe day with shear still in place. It is uncertain if dry air \nslowly advecting in will erode away at the instability prior to \nreaching the convective temperature in the afternoon. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nWith a stalled front and high precipitable water values \ntomorrow into Thursday morning of around 2 inches, heavy rain \nand flash flooding is a concern. Now that more of the CAMs are \nwithin range, the HREF's probability matched mean which helps \nshow some reasonable worst case scenarios does highlight an area\nfrom CNY down into the Catskills/southern Hudson River Valley \nwhere a strip of 2 to 3 inches of rain could fall in 12 hours. A\nlow level jet feeding into the front Wendesday night into \nThursday morning will help feed training storms if the front \nremains stalled all night so pockets of 3+ inches is expected. \nFlash flooding is likely where the front ends up stalling with \npotential for isolated significant flash flooding.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nFor Friday into early next week, the large ridge in the central\nUS retrogrades west with the trough in the northeast nudging \ninto the mid Atlantic. This will help the flow become more \nnortherly with cooler and drier air advecting in. Depending on \nhow cool the 850 mb temperatures are, there could be some lake \neffect rain showers to deal with but for the most part the \nweekend into early next week is looking mostly dry.",
